analysisWe need to produce an assert statement that matches the comment.

The comment:

```
// @@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to the initial size of the vector, then for every valid index i from the insertion point to the previous size of the vector, the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and the corresponding old element at position i is null, or it is equal to the old element at position i.
```

We need to translate to a formal assertion.

We have method insertElementAt(E obj, int index). It inserts obj at index, shifting elements from index onward one position to the right.

The comment says: If index >=0 && index <= old size (initial size) then for every valid index i from insertion point to previous size of vector, the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and old element at i is null, or equal to old element at i.

Essentially for i in [index, oldSize-1] (since previous size = old size before insertion), we have:

(this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) || (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null))

But we need to use observer methods available: elementAt(int index) returns element at index. Also we have get(int index) maybe but synchronized version. In this class, elementAt is synchronized (public synchronized E elementAt(int index)). So we can use this.elementAt(i+1). Also we need \old(this.elementAt(i)). However elementAt may throw if out of range; but we are within valid range after insertion.

We also need to capture old size: \old(this.size()).

Premise: index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size()).

Then assert: (index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) );

Need to handle null equality: use == null for null; for non-null use .equals. Since elementAt returns E, which could be null; .equals would cause NPE if null. So we need to write condition with null check.

Hence spec: (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || (this.elementAt(i+1) != null && this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))).

Simplify: (this.elementAt(i+1) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))).

But typical spec uses explicit disjunction.

Thus final assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || (this.elementAt(i+1) != null && this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) );

Make sure parentheses correct. Use && inside forall condition.

Thus final answer code block.

assistantfinal<code>assert (index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => 
       \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); 
           ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) ||
             (this.elementAt(i+1) != null && this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) );</code>
